    expectedAnswer: "React 19 Form Actions allow a function to be passed to form action so submission can be modeled as an Action. This integrates naturally with FormData, pending state and action result handling through hooks such as useActionState and useFormStatus. Server-side frameworks can extend this model with Server Actions.",
    deepExplanation: "```tsx\nimport { useActionState } from \"react\";\n\ntype Result = {\n  message: string;\n};\n\nasync function saveProfile(\n  _previous: Result,\n  formData: FormData,\n): Promise<Result> {\n  const name = String(formData.get(\"name\") ?? \"\").trim();\n\n  if (!name) {\n    return { message: \"Name is required\" };\n  }\n\n  await saveToServer(name);\n  return { message: \"Profile saved\" };\n}\n\nfunction ProfileForm() {\n  const [result, action, pending] =\n    useActionState(saveProfile, { message: \"\" });\n\n  return (\n    <form action={action}>\n      <label>\n        Name\n        <input name=\"name\" />\n      </label>\n\n      <button disabled={pending}>\n        {pending ? \"Saving…\" : \"Save\"}\n      </button>\n\n      <p role=\"status\">{result.message}</p>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThe important distinction is that the form supplies `FormData`; the Action owns the mutation workflow.",
